#: ../../../MemPGO.md:1
msgid "MemPGO: Profile-Guided Memory Optimization for LLVM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:4
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:6
msgid ""
"**MemPGO** (**Profile-Guided Memory Optimization**) is LLVM's profile-guided "
"optimization (PGO) framework for memory. It uses **MemProf** (**Memory "
"Profiling**)—LLVM's heap and data-access profiling instrumentation, runtime, "
"and profile format—together with compiler feedback passes to optimize "
"dynamic memory allocations and static data layout based on runtime profiling "
"information. For heap allocations, MemProf attributes memory-access and "
"lifetime statistics to each allocation's **allocation context**—the full "
"dynamic call stack leading to the allocation. (In LLVM flags, IR metadata, "
"and pass names such as `-fmemory-profile`, `!memprof`, and `MemProfUse`, the "
"framework and its components are also referred to as `MemProf`.) By "
"understanding the \"hotness\", lifetime, and access frequency of memory "
"across calling contexts, the compiler can make better decisions about where "
"to place data (both heap and static), reducing fragmentation and improving "
"cache locality."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:8
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:10
msgid ""
"Traditional PGO focuses on control flow (hot vs. cold code). MemPGO extends "
"this concept to data. It answers questions like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:12
msgid "Which allocation sites are \"hot\" (frequently accessed)?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:13
msgid "Which allocation sites are \"cold\" (rarely accessed)?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:14
msgid "What is the lifetime of an allocation?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:16
msgid "This information enables optimizations such as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:18
msgid ""
"**Heap Layout Optimization:** Grouping objects with similar lifetimes or "
"access density. This currently requires an allocator that supports the "
"necessary interfaces (e.g., tcmalloc)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:19
msgid ""
"**Static Data Partitioning:** Segregating frequently accessed (hot) global "
"variables and constants from rarely accessed (cold) ones to improve data "
"locality and TLB utilization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:21
msgid "User Manual"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:23
msgid ""
"This section describes how to use MemPGO to profile and optimize your "
"application."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:25
msgid "Building with MemProf Instrumentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:27
msgid ""
"To enable MemProf instrumentation, compile your application with the `-"
"fmemory-profile` flag. Make sure to include debug information (`-gmlt` and `-"
"fdebug-info-for-profiling`) and frame pointers to ensure accurate stack "
"traces and line number reporting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:34
msgid ""
"Link with `-fmemory-profile` as well to link the necessary runtime "
"libraries. If you use a separate link step, ensure the flag is passed to the "
"linker. On Linux, the flags `-Wl,-z,noseparate-code -Wl,--build-id` are "
"currently required to ensure the binary layout (executable segment at offset "
"0) and Build ID presence are compatible with the `llvm-profdata` profile "
"reader."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:38
msgid "Running and Generating Profiles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:40
msgid ""
"Run the instrumented application. By default, MemProf writes a raw profile "
"file named `memprof.profraw.<pid>` to the current directory upon exit. "
"Control the runtime behavior using the `MEMPROF_OPTIONS` environment "
"variable. Common options include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:42
msgid ""
"`log_path`: Redirects runtime logs (e.g., `stdout`, `stderr`, or a file "
"path)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:43
msgid ""
"`print_text`: If set to `true`, prints a text-based summary of the profile "
"to the log path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../MemPGO.md:44
msgid "`verbosity`: Controls the level of debug output."
msgstr ""

